## Course overview

English A: Language and Literature develops a close, critical understanding of how texts work - both literary works and the everyday language of media, advertising, and public life. Students learn to read analytically, write persuasively, and speak with confidence about meaning, audience, and context.

## What you will study

- **Readers, writers and texts**: how meaning is built through language and form across a range of text types.
- **Time and space**: how context of production and reception shapes a text.
- **Intertextuality**: how texts connect, echo, and respond to one another.
- A balanced selection of **literary works** and **non-literary body of texts** (journalism, speeches, advertising, digital media).

## Assessment

| Component | SL | HL | Description |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Paper 1 (guided textual analysis) | 35% | 35% | Analysis of previously unseen texts |
| Paper 2 (comparative essay) | 35% | 25% | Comparison of two studied literary works |
| HL Essay | - | 20% | 1,200-1,500 word essay on one text |
| Individual Oral | 30% | 20% | Spoken analysis linking a literary and non-literary text |

The Higher and Standard Level courses share the same aims; HL studies more works and adds the written HL Essay.
